I have been wondering about the strange way NH3.0 (PL9) defines the need of
food.

I assumed that NetHack used a very intelligent way to determine how much
food is consumed by the player. Basically I thought the quantity of food used
was based on:
0. time (one always need food even for basic function as breathing).
1. wearing rings & amulets (the more wearing the more food it costs)
2. casting spells, jumping and kicking etc may cost extra energy.
3. total weight my character is carrying/dragging through the dungeon
   (the lighter one travels the less food consumed)
4. whether one is actually moving or just waiting/sleeping. Not walking
   takes up less energy, so less food consumption.
5. using special tools could increase the use of food, e.g. digging with
   a pick-axe seems to me to cost much more energy (thus food) than just
   walking around.

As the explanation of the actual way NetHack deals with this this may be a
spoiler, so there is now a <<SPOILER ALERT>>

Peeking in the source code I noticed that only assumptions 0, 1 and 2 are
true and the others are false. This sounds very weird to me !! 
From the food point of view there is no distinction between the case in which
I am standing still (eg waiting for my cat/dog to go downstairs) while carring
nothing and the case in which I almost collapse under load while digging my
own way with a pick-age.

Shouldn't this be changed ? Any ideas ? Other opinions ? David ? Dev Team ?
